<section class="content-section" id="introduction">

    <h2>2. Introduction to Detection Engineering</h2>

    <p>
        Security systems generate thousands or millions of events.
        These events may include login attempts, process execution,
        network connections, file modifications and administrative actions.
    </p>

    <p>
        However, collecting events is not the same as identifying threats.
        Security teams need detection logic that identifies meaningful
        patterns in the available data.
    </p>

    <p>
        Detection Engineering is the process of designing, implementing,
        testing and improving rules that identify potentially malicious
        or suspicious activity.
    </p>

    <h3>Example Scenario</h3>

    <p>
        Imagine an employee account normally logs in from one office
        computer. Suddenly, the account performs several failed login
        attempts followed by a successful login from an unfamiliar system.
    </p>

    <p>
        A detection engineer may build a rule that identifies repeated
        authentication failures followed by a successful login.
    </p>

    <div class="info-card">
        <p>
            <strong>Important:</strong>
            A detection alert is an indication that activity deserves
            investigation. It is not automatic proof of a cyberattack.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Detection Engineering vs Traditional Monitoring</h3>

    <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table>
            <tr>
                <th>Traditional Monitoring</th>
                <th>Detection Engineering</th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Collects system events.</td>
                <td>Develops logic to identify suspicious behaviour.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Displays dashboards and alerts.</td>
                <td>Tests whether alerts identify relevant activity.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>May rely on default rules.</td>
                <td>Creates and continuously improves detection rules.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Focuses on visibility.</td>
                <td>Focuses on actionable security evidence.</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </div>

</section>

<section class="content-section" id="fundamentals">

    <h2>3. Detection Fundamentals</h2>

    <h3>What Is a Detection Rule?</h3>

    <p>
        A detection rule is a set of conditions used to identify
        activity that may indicate a security problem.
    </p>

    <p>A basic detection rule generally contains:</p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Data Source:</strong> Where the event originates.</li>
        <li><strong>Event Selection:</strong> Which events are relevant.</li>
        <li><strong>Conditions:</strong> What behaviour should be identified.</li>
        <li><strong>Threshold:</strong> How frequently an event must occur.</li>
        <li><strong>Alert:</strong> What happens when conditions are met.</li>
        <li><strong>Context:</strong> Information needed by an analyst.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common Detection Categories</h3>

    <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table>
            <tr>
                <th>Category</th>
                <th>Example Activity</th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Authentication</td>
                <td>Repeated failed login attempts.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Endpoint</td>
                <td>Unexpected process execution.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Network</td>
                <td>Unexpected outbound connections.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Privilege</td>
                <td>Unexpected administrative activity.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Persistence</td>
                <td>Unexpected scheduled task creation.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Data Access</td>
                <td>Unusual access to sensitive resources.</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </div>

</section>

<section class="content-section" id="telemetry">

    <h2>4. Understanding Security Telemetry</h2>

    <p>
        Telemetry is the information generated by systems and security
        devices that helps security teams understand what happened.
    </p>

    <h3>Common Telemetry Sources</h3>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Windows Event Logs:</strong> Authentication and system activity.</li>
        <li><strong>Linux Logs:</strong> Authentication, processes and system events.</li>
        <li><strong>Firewall Logs:</strong> Allowed and blocked network traffic.</li>
        <li><strong>DNS Logs:</strong> Domain lookup activity.</li>
        <li><strong>EDR Data:</strong> Endpoint processes and behaviour.</li>
        <li><strong>Web Server Logs:</strong> HTTP requests and response information.</li>
        <li><strong>Cloud Audit Logs:</strong> Cloud resource and account activity.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example Security Event</h3>

    <div class="code-block">
<pre>{
  "timestamp": "2026-01-15T10:30:00Z",
  "hostname": "WORKSTATION-01",
  "username": "analyst01",
  "event_type": "authentication",
  "event_id": 4625,
  "source_ip": "192.0.2.45",
  "status": "failure"
}</pre>
    </div>

    <p>
        This synthetic event represents a failed Windows authentication
        attempt. Event ID 4625 is associated with failed logon events.
    </p>

    <h3>Why Data Quality Matters</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Missing timestamps make event correlation difficult.</li>
        <li>Incorrect time zones affect investigation timelines.</li>
        <li>Missing usernames reduce attribution capabilities.</li>
        <li>Inconsistent field names make queries unreliable.</li>
        <li>Incomplete event collection creates detection blind spots.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warning-card">
        <p>
            <strong>Detection limitation:</strong>
            A detection rule cannot reliably identify activity that its
            underlying data source does not record or collect.
        </p>
    </div>

</section>

<section class="content-section" id="lifecycle">

    <h2>5. Detection Engineering Lifecycle</h2>

    <p>
        Detection engineering is a continuous improvement process.
        A rule should not be considered complete simply because it
        generates an alert.
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li><strong>Understand the Threat:</strong> Identify the behaviour you want to detect.</li>
        <li><strong>Identify Telemetry:</strong> Determine which logs contain relevant evidence.</li>
        <li><strong>Develop Logic:</strong> Define the detection conditions.</li>
        <li><strong>Implement:</strong> Write the rule or SIEM query.</li>
        <li><strong>Test:</strong> Validate against representative sample events.</li>
        <li><strong>Evaluate:</strong> Review true positives and false positives.</li>
        <li><strong>Document:</strong> Record assumptions, coverage and limitations.</li>
        <li><strong>Improve:</strong> Update the detection as environments change.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="info-card">
        <p>
            <strong>Engineering Principle:</strong>
            A detection rule must be testable, explainable, maintainable
            and supported by observable evidence.
        </p>
    </div>

</section>

<section class="content-section" id="mitre">

    <h2>6. MITRE ATT&CK Mapping</h2>

    <p>
        <a href="https://attack.mitre.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            MITRE ATT&CK
        </a>
        is a knowledge base that describes adversary tactics and techniques.
        It helps security teams understand and classify attacker behaviour.
    </p>

    <h3>Important Terminology</h3>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Tactic:</strong> The attacker's objective.</li>
        <li><strong>Technique:</strong> The method used to achieve that objective.</li>
        <li><strong>Sub-technique:</strong> A more specific variation of a technique.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example Mapping</h3>

    <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table>
            <tr>
                <th>Activity</th>
                <th>ATT&CK Technique</th>
                <th>ID</th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Brute-force attempts</td>
                <td>Brute Force</td>
                <td>T1110</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Suspicious PowerShell execution</td>
                <td>PowerShell</td>
                <td>T1059.001</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Unusual scheduled task creation</td>
                <td>Scheduled Task/Job</td>
                <td>T1053</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Credential dumping behaviour</td>
                <td>OS Credential Dumping</td>
                <td>T1003</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p>
        Mapping a detection to ATT&CK helps communicate what behaviour
        the rule is designed to identify. It does not establish that
        the entire technique is covered.
    </p>

</section>

<section class="content-section" id="sigma">

    <h2>7. Introduction to Sigma Rules</h2>

    <p>
        Sigma is an open detection-rule format that allows security
        analysts and engineers to describe log-based detection logic
        in a platform-independent way.
    </p>

    <p>
        Sigma rules can be converted into queries for supported
        security platforms using compatible conversion tools.
    </p>

    <h3>Basic Sigma Rule Structure</h3>

    <div class="code-block">
<pre>title: Multiple Failed Authentication Attempts
id: 11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111
status: experimental

description: >
  Identifies failed authentication events
  for further investigation.

logsource:
    product: windows
    service: security

detection:
    selection:
        EventID: 4625

    condition: selection

level: medium

tags:
    - attack.credential-access
    - attack.t1110</pre>
    </div>

    <h3>Understanding the Fields</h3>

    <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table>
            <tr>
                <th>Field</th>
                <th>Purpose</th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>title</td>
                <td>Human-readable detection name.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>id</td>
                <td>Unique identifier for the rule.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>status</td>
                <td>Development or validation status.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>description</td>
                <td>Explains the detection purpose.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>logsource</td>
                <td>Describes the relevant event source.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>detection</td>
                <td>Contains the selection and matching conditions.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>level</td>
                <td>Indicates the rule's severity classification.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>tags</td>
                <td>Provides classification and ATT&CK mapping.</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="warning-card">
        <p>
            <strong>Important:</strong>
            This is an illustrative starter rule, not a complete
            brute-force detection. It identifies individual failed
            authentication events. Correlation, thresholds, field
            mapping and testing must be implemented separately.
        </p>
    </div>

</section>

<section class="content-section" id="siem">

    <h2>8. Detection Using SIEM Queries</h2>

    <p>
        Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) platforms
        collect and analyse events from different systems.
    </p>

    <p>
        Detection engineers use query languages to search, filter,
        correlate and investigate this information.
    </p>

    <h3>Example: Splunk SPL</h3>

    <div class="code-block">
<pre>index=windows
EventCode=4625
| stats count by user, src_ip
| where count >= 5
| sort - count</pre>
    </div>

    <p>
        This illustrative query groups failed logon events by username
        and source IP and displays groups containing five or more events.
    </p>

    <h3>Example: Microsoft Sentinel KQL</h3>

    <div class="code-block">
<pre>SecurityEvent
| where EventID == 4625
| summarize FailedAttempts = count()
    by Account, IpAddress, bin(TimeGenerated, 10m)
| where FailedAttempts >= 5
| order by FailedAttempts desc</pre>
    </div>

    <p>
        This query groups failed logon events into ten-minute intervals
        and identifies groups with at least five failures.
    </p>

    <div class="info-card">
        <p>
            <strong>Platform dependency:</strong>
            Field names, data availability and query behaviour depend
            on the SIEM schema and log ingestion configuration.
        </p>
    </div>

</section>

<section class="content-section" id="false-positives">

    <h2>9. False Positives and Detection Quality</h2>

    <p>
        A false positive occurs when a detection identifies activity
        as suspicious even though investigation shows that the activity
        is legitimate.
    </p>

    <p>
        For example, five failed logins may result from an employee
        entering an incorrect password repeatedly.
    </p>

    <h3>Detection Outcomes</h3>

    <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table>
            <tr>
                <th>Outcome</th>
                <th>Meaning</th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>True Positive</td>
                <td>A relevant security event was correctly identified.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>False Positive</td>
                <td>Legitimate activity generated an incorrect alert.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>True Negative</td>
                <td>Normal activity was correctly not alerted.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>False Negative</td>
                <td>A relevant security event was missed.</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Ways to Improve Detection Quality</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Use appropriate event thresholds.</li>
        <li>Include relevant user and asset context.</li>
        <li>Validate detection logic against legitimate activity.</li>
        <li>Review repeated alert patterns.</li>
        <li>Document approved exceptions.</li>
        <li>Test rule changes before production deployment.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warning-card">
        <p>
            <strong>Trade-off:</strong>
            Making a rule too restrictive can reduce false positives
            while increasing false negatives. Detection tuning must
            balance both risks.
        </p>
    </div>

</section>

<section class="content-section" id="practical">

    <h2>10. Practical Labs</h2>

    <p>
        The following exercises use synthetic security events.
        Perform them in a controlled learning environment.
        Do not generate unauthorized authentication attempts or
        access third-party systems.
    </p>

    <div class="task-card">

        <h3>Lab 01: Security Event Analysis</h3>

        <p><strong>Objective:</strong> Understand authentication event patterns.</p>

        <p><strong>Sample dataset:</strong></p>

        <div class="code-block">
<pre>timestamp,user,source_ip,event_id,status
10:00:01,admin,192.0.2.10,4625,failure
10:00:05,admin,192.0.2.10,4625,failure
10:00:09,admin,192.0.2.10,4625,failure
10:01:10,analyst,192.0.2.20,4624,success
10:02:00,admin,192.0.2.10,4624,success
10:05:00,user01,192.0.2.30,4625,failure</pre>
        </div>

        <p><strong>Tasks:</strong></p>

        <ol>
            <li>Identify failed authentication events.</li>
            <li>Count failures for each user.</li>
            <li>Identify repeated failures from one source IP.</li>
            <li>Find whether a successful login followed the failures.</li>
            <li>Document what additional information an analyst needs.</li>
        </ol>

