<section class="content-section" id="investigation">

    <h2>11. Detection Investigation and Reporting</h2>

    <p>
        Detection development does not end when a rule generates an alert.
        Engineers must document what the rule detects, how it works and
        what an analyst should investigate.
    </p>

    <h3>Recommended Detection Documentation</h3>

    <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table>
            <tr>
                <th>Field</th>
                <th>Required Information</th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Detection Name</td>
                <td>Unique and understandable name.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Objective</td>
                <td>What behaviour is being detected.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Data Source</td>
                <td>Relevant event source and fields.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Detection Logic</td>
                <td>Rule or query implementation.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>ATT&CK Mapping</td>
                <td>Relevant tactic and technique.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Testing</td>
                <td>Test dataset and validation results.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Limitations</td>
                <td>Known gaps and assumptions.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Response Guidance</td>
                <td>Recommended investigation steps.</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Investigation Questions</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Which user or system generated the event?</li>
        <li>When did the activity occur?</li>
        <li>Which source IP or endpoint was involved?</li>
        <li>Were there related events before or after the alert?</li>
        <li>Does the behaviour match expected business activity?</li>
        <li>What additional evidence is required?</li>
    </ul>

</section>

